Fluoroquinolone-Induced Achilles Tendinopathy and Tendon Rupture.

  ## 1. Case Scenario and Patient Narrative

### Initial Information
* Report Type: Initial Spontaneous Report
* Country of Origin: India
* Primary Source: Healthcare Professional (Orthopedic Surgeon / Physician)

### Patient Demographics
* Patient Initials: R.B.
* Age: 68 years
* Gender: Male
* Weight: 76 kg
* Relevant Medical History: Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD), mild osteoarthritis. No prior history of Achilles tendinopathy or steroid use.

### Suspected and Concomitant Drugs
* Suspected Drug: Levofloxacin 500 mg orally once daily (Indication: Acute Bacterial Exacerbation of COPD; Duration: Initiated 10 days prior to emergency admission).
* Interacting Drug: None.
* Concomitant Drugs: Tiotropium inhaler 18 mcg daily, Salbutamol inhaler as needed.

### Case Narrative and Timeline
* Day 1: Patient initiated on oral Levofloxacin 500 mg daily for acute exacerbation of COPD.
* Day 8: Patient developed sudden bilateral posterior ankle pain, swelling, and morning stiffness upon weight-bearing.
* Day 10: Experienced a sudden "popping" sensation in the right heel while walking, followed by immediate inability to plantarfles or bear weight on the right leg. Presented to an orthopedic emergency clinic in India.
* Diagnostic Findings at Admission:
  * Physical Exam: Positive Thompson Squeeze Test on the right leg (absent plantar flexion); palpable gap 4 cm proximal to calcaneal insertion.
  * Ankle Ultrasound: Complete full-thickness tear of the right Achilles tendon and high-grade tendinopathy of the left Achilles tendon.
  * Systemic Markers: Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP) within normal reference limits.
* Action Taken: Levofloxacin was immediately and permanently discontinued. Patient underwent emergency open orthopedic surgical repair of the right Achilles tendon and casting/immobilization of the left ankle.
* Outcome: At Month 4 post-discontinuation, the right Achilles tendon healed post-repair, left tendinopathy resolved fully without secondary rupture, and mobility was restored.

